Yes—for the root failure class that allowed Hive Mind itself to turn a confirmed merge into exit code 1, this is now fixed in [`adb16e33`](https://github.com/link-assistant/hive-mind/commit/adb16e33).

The original implementation kept merge success only as a return value. Any later cleanup/logging/diagnostic exception could still reach an unconditional exit-1 handler, and `solve` had two pairs of process-error listeners that could race. The fix now:

- latches the confirmed merge before the first post-merge `await`;
- makes every later internal nonzero exit preserve status 0 and log the error;
- skips failure side effects after that terminal success;
- removes the duplicate handler pair; and
- isolates each finalization step.

The regression uses real child processes: pre-merge `safeExit(1)` remains 1, post-merge `safeExit(1)` becomes 0, exception/rejection paths remain 0 even if diagnostic logging fails, and injected cleanup/Sentry/handle failures still reach success. It passes 16/16 on Node 20 and Node 24, and the current-main suite passes all 357 test files.

One evidence boundary is important: the retained historical log ends almost three minutes before the merge, and the original container/final outer log were gone, so the exact statement that threw in that 2026-07-30 run cannot be named honestly. The reproducible lifecycle defect that made the contradiction possible is closed. An uncatchable SIGKILL/OOM/host removal cannot be converted by Node code; those external events remain observable, with the Telegram fallback reporting both the merged PR and runner failure instead of claiming the whole task failed.
